# Line endings: LF in the repository, LF in every working tree.
#
# ─── Why this file exists ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM config, so a Windows
# checkout gets CRLF in the working tree while the blobs stay LF. Prettier's
# endOfLine default is "lf". The two disagree, and the result was that
# `pnpm format:check` reported 1787 files locally against the 3 that CI saw —
# 1784 of them purely about line endings.
#
# A check that is wrong 99.8% of the time is not a check. It gets ignored, or
# it gets "fixed" with a `prettier --write` across the whole tree. In two days
# it let two prettier breakages onto master (#74/#76, then #78), and since
# branch protection went on, a red Lint + Format blocks every merge.
#
# `eol=lf` — not just `text=auto` — because the working tree is what tools
# read. Measured on apps/api/drizzle/0000_baseline.sql:
#
#   sha256 of the blob        (LF, what CI computes)   5b7c4cf24eb14502…
#   sha256 of the working copy (CRLF, what Windows had) 5aed7514f312d563…
#
# drizzle-orm/migrator.js:23 stores exactly that sha256 in __drizzle_migrations,
# so the same migration hashed differently depending on which OS ran it.
#
# The repository itself needed no conversion: every text blob was already LF
# (checked with `git grep -I -l -P '\r' HEAD`, which found none, while the same
# expression against the working tree found 1988 files). This pins that state
# rather than changing it.

# Everything is text unless git's own detection says otherwise; store LF, and
# check out LF on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ─── Exceptions ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The two binary files in the repo. Git's auto-detection would classify both
# correctly (each has NUL bytes inside the first 8 KB), but both also contain
# raw CR bytes — 1067 in the PDF, 4251 in the zip — so if the detection were
# ever wrong, normalisation would corrupt them silently. Saying it outright
# costs two lines.
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary

# Shell scripts: LF is not a preference here. A CRLF shebang makes Linux report
# "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M" and the script does not run at all. Both
# tracked scripts (scripts/apply-migrations.sh, scripts/dev-stack.sh) are LF
# today; this keeps them that way even if someone commits from an editor that
# does not care.
*.sh text eol=lf

# SQL migrations: covered by the rule above, restated because the consequence
# is not cosmetic. drizzle hashes the file contents, so a CRLF copy of an
# already-applied migration reads as a different migration.
*.sql text eol=lf

# No `eol=crlf` entries: the repo tracks no .bat, .cmd, .ps1 or .sln, so
# nothing here wants CRLF in the working tree.
